WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.375
Capacity planning should begin with measurements from each backend.

00:00:03.375 --> 00:00:07.660
Useful signals include prompt processing speed, generation speed, time to first token,

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:13.210
total response time, active requests, queue length, CPU use, GPU use, VRAM pressure,

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.050
memory pressure, and failure rate.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.660
Tokens per second is useful, but it does not describe the whole user experience.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.520
Context length changes performance.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.625
A long document prompt can consume far more memory and processing time than a short chat

00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:25.084
message.

00:00:25.084 --> 00:00:29.150
A service may benchmark well with small prompts and then struggle with real document

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:30.930
analysis or coding-agent sessions.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.780
Testing should include the actual types of work the load-balanced endpoint is expected to

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:35.190
serve.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.985
Concurrency limits protect the service from slow collapse.

00:00:37.985 --> 00:00:41.825
Every backend reaches a point where adding more simultaneous requests makes everyone wait

00:00:41.825 --> 00:00:43.380
longer or causes failures.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.535
Runtime batching and parallelism can help in some environments, but they need measurement.

00:00:47.535 --> 00:00:51.400
More concurrency is not automatically better if it increases time to first token or causes

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.660
out-of-memory behavior.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.295
Warm and cold states matter.

00:00:54.295 --> 00:00:58.360
A model already loaded in memory can respond much faster than one being read from disk.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.130
A freshly restarted server may be online but not ready.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:05.105
Capacity planning should combine benchmark data with representative workloads: chat,

00:01:05.105 --> 00:01:08.540
coding assistants, batch jobs, long contexts, and mixed traffic.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.880
The goal is to know when to route, queue, shed load, or ask clients to retry later.
